[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Entity Mapping, Basic Annotations, and Column Configurations in Hibernate"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hibernate is a powerful Object-Relational Mapping (ORM) framework for Java. It simplifies database interactions by mapping Java classes to database tables and Java properties to table columns. This tutorial covers the foundational annotations required to map basic entities and configure their columns properly."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. Defining an Entity"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To mark a standard Java class as a database entity, you must use the @Entity annotation. By default, Hibernate maps the class to a table with the same name. You can override this behavior using the @Table annotation to specify a custom table name and schema."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"import jakarta.persistence.Entity;
import jakarta.persistence.Table;

@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    // Entity fields go here
}"},{"order":6,"type":"heading","text":"2. Primary Key Mapping with @Id and @GeneratedValue"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Every entity must have a primary key. The @Id annotation specifies the primary key property of an entity. To let the database or Hibernate handle ID generation automatically, you combine it with the @GeneratedValue annotation."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"import jakarta.persistence.Id;
import jakarta.persistence.GeneratedValue;
import jakarta.persistence.GenerationType;

@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"3. Column Configurations using @Column"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The @Column annotation is used to configure the mapping between a Java object field and a database column. If omitted, Hibernate applies sensible defaults (e.g., column name matches field name, nullable is true)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"name: Specifies the column name in the database table."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"nullable: Specifies whether the column can contain null values (default: true)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"unique: Ensures that all values in the column are unique (default: false)."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"length: Defines the column length for string types (default: 255)."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@Column(name = "email_address", nullable = false, unique = true, length = 100)
private String email;"},{"order":16,"type":"heading","text":"4. Handling Transient and Date Fields"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you have fields in your entity class that should not be persisted to the database. You can use the @Transient annotation for this purpose. For temporal types like dates and timestamps, the @Temporal annotation is used to specify the exact database temporal type."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"import jakarta.persistence.Transient;
import jakarta.persistence.Temporal;
import jakarta.persistence.TemporalType;
import java.util.Date;

@Temporal(TemporalType.TIMESTAMP)
@Column(name = "created_at", updatable = false)
private Date createdAt;

@Transient
private String temporarySessionToken;"},{"order":19,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You have learned how to map a Java class to a database table using @Entity, define primary keys with @Id and @GeneratedValue, and fine-tune column properties using @Column. These foundational annotations form the backbone of any data model built with Hibernate and JPA."}]
